## 7. Advanced Interaction and Animation

Use motion as an essential part of the brand identity, not as decoration added everywhere.

Implement the following where appropriate:

1. Smooth page transitions.
2. Staggered hero typography reveals.
3. Image masking and reveal animations.
4. Subtle image zoom on hover.
5. Scroll-triggered section reveals.
6. A restrained parallax effect on selected photographs.
7. Horizontal gallery movement.
8. Animated navigation indicators.
9. Smooth lightbox transitions.
10. Animated collection titles.
11. A custom cursor on desktop, only if it improves usability.
12. A minimal scroll-progress indicator.
13. Smooth return-to-top behavior.
14. Micro-interactions on buttons and links.
15. Loading placeholders or progressive image loading.

Technical requirements:
- Prefer performant CSS transforms and opacity animations.
- Use Intersection Observer or an appropriate animation library for scroll-triggered effects.
- Prevent horizontal overflow caused by animations.
- Do not hijack normal scrolling.
- Avoid continuous animation that drains battery or distracts from photography.
- Disable or simplify nonessential motion for users who prefer reduced motion.
- Keep navigation and important content usable before animations finish.

If using a library such as GSAP or Framer Motion, use it purposefully and avoid unnecessary dependencies.

