## 9. Responsive Design

Build mobile-first, then refine the desktop experience.

Desktop:
- Cinematic hero compositions.
- Large editorial typography.
- Wide image layouts.
- Asymmetrical grids.
- Sophisticated hover effects.

Tablet:
- Simplified editorial grids.
- Adjusted headline sizes.
- Reduced image overlap.
- Comfortable spacing and navigation.

Mobile:
- Compact navigation with a working menu.
- Properly cropped portrait photography.
- Readable typography.
- Single-column editorial sections where necessary.
- Touch-friendly controls.
- Swipe-friendly gallery navigation where appropriate.
- No hover-only interactions.
- No accidental horizontal overflow.
- Fast image loading.

Test the layouts at approximately 360px, 390px, 768px, 1024px, and 1440px widths.

Do not simply shrink the desktop layout. Recompose sections intentionally for smaller screens.

## 10. Photography and Visual Assets

Fashion photography is one of the most important elements of this project.

Use high-quality, editorial-style fashion imagery with:
- Avant-garde garments.
- Architectural silhouettes.
- Experimental styling.
- Dramatic studio lighting.
- Interesting textures.
- Strong model poses.
- Artistic compositions.
- Monochrome imagery mixed with selective color.
- Diverse models and believable fashion campaigns.

Use real image assets from sources you can access and are permitted to use, or generate original visuals if the platform supports it.

Suitable sources may include licensed stock photography, approved image libraries, and original AI-generated fashion imagery.

Do not use:
- Broken image URLs.
- Random unrelated stock photography.
- Repeated copies of the same image across every section.
- Low-resolution thumbnails as full-screen hero images.
- Images that look like generic corporate business stock.
- Images that misrepresent actual brand collaborators or designers.

Assign each image a specific role: hero, full-length look, garment detail, campaign, material study, or behind-the-scenes image.

Use consistent image ratios where needed, while retaining varied editorial compositions.

Configure descriptive alt text, appropriate object positioning, lazy loading below the fold, and sensible responsive image sizes.

